Chapter: ProcessExitStatus

 The unary process-exit means something on bare metal. A
 child that calls process-exit N and a child that returns N both leave
 N in the slot's exit-status cell -- the unary helper stores its
 argument, and __proc_entry stores the closure's return value before
 joining the exit path. The exit wake loop delivers the status into a
 blocked waiter's saved RAX, and process-wait's already-dead path reads
 the cell directly, so process-wait answers the child's status
 whichever side of the exit race the waiter lands on. The two children
 use different constants so a wait that answers the wrong child's
 status, or a stale zero, cannot pass.

Section: Children

  ret-child : Integer -> Integer
  ret-child (x) = 40 + x

  exit-child : Integer -> [Process] Integer
  exit-child (x) = act
    process-exit (60 + x)
    let unreached = 0
    in unreached
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Concurrent, Process] Nothing = act
    p1 <- process-spawn (\x -> ret-child x)
    let w1 = process-wait p1
    in act
      print-line-uni ("ret-status: " & show w1)
      p2 <- process-spawn (\x -> exit-child x)
      let w2 = process-wait p2
      in print-line-uni ("exit-status: " & show w2)
    end
  end
